              "poles": "betelgeuse-poles-matisse@2x.webp",
              "source": {
                "id": "betelgeuse-matisse-2020-02-continuum",
                "path": "../manifest.json"
              },
              "falseColor": true,
              "notes": "An image of the photosphere reconstructed by the public SQUEEZE code from public VLT/MATISSE visibilities and closure phases of 8 and 19 February 2020, in the 3.94-4.00 micrometre continuum, convolved to the 4 milliarcsecond interferometric beam and cast onto a sphere of the published radius, so nothing finer than the beam is shown. Its bright and dark patches are not confirmed as surface features: a spotless disc reconstructed from the same coverage, at sizes within 2 percent of the star's, leaves patches up to as strong. Negating every closure phase fits equally well, so which limb is brighter is weakly constrained. The colours are a legend for reconstructed relative intensity in one infrared band, the paper's heat scale: not colour, temperature or albedo. A fifth of the reconstruction's flux lies outside the disc, and it is drawn here in three dimensions rather than as a flat plate behind the sphere: the envelope that best projects to its measured radial profile, which falls as r to the minus eight. The ring within one beam of the limb is left out, because there the light is the star's own edge smeared by the 4 milliarcsecond beam rather than anything resolved; that is about half of the off-limb flux. What is drawn is the extended emission the visibilities require. The far hemisphere and the poles were not observed.",
