                      240
                    ]
                  ],
                  "labels": [
                    "0.35",
                    "0.68",
                    "1.00"
                  ]
                },
                "meta": "fraction of the brightest point · 1st–99.5th percentile stretch",
                "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.12404"
              },
              "volume": {
                "objectId": "betelgeuse-shell",
                "lensId": "emission-2020",
                "surface": "matisse"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dust-2024",
              "label": "Polarised dust · December 2024",
              "thumbnail": "betelgeuse-lens-matisse.webp",
              "volume": {
                "objectId": "betelgeuse-shell",
                "lensId": "zimpol-v",
                "surface": "matisse"
              },
              "notes": "The photosphere as the MATISSE reconstruction shows it, with the dust around it: the degree of linear polarisation VLT/SPHERE-ZIMPOL measured in the V band on 3 December 2024, released by ESO as Phase 3 collection BETELGEUSE-B beside Montargès et al. (2026). The patches one to about 4.5 stellar radii out are starlight scattered by dust. This is their own Fig. B.1 given depth: the same inferno colour map on the same zero-to-0.10 scale, carried as four emission channels so a line of sight emits the bar colour of its own measured degree. The colours are a legend for a ratio, not the colour of the dust. The instrumental polarisation measured beyond eight radii is subtracted, so the empty sky is darker here than in the printed figure. The map is a sky-plane image with no third axis, so the depth is inferred rather than pushed back from the sky: the azimuthally averaged radial profile is fitted with simple envelopes around the star, and the one that projects to it is a spherical shell of 3.5 stellar radii and gaussian thickness 1.2, which beats both a steady outflow and the constant depth an extrusion assumes. Each patch is placed on that shell, in front of the star and behind it, because nothing in one image says which. That shape is an inference from the profile, not a measurement.",
              "falseColor": true,
              "legend": {
                "kind": "scale",
                "title": "Degree of linear polarisation",
                "labels": [
                  "0.00",
                  "0.05",
                  "0.10"
                ],
                "recipe": {
                  "palette": [
                    [
                      0,
