                      252,
                      255,
                      164
                    ]
                  ],
                  "labels": [
                    "0.00",
                    "0.05",
                    "0.10"
                  ]
                },
                "meta": "V band, 3 December 2024 · the paper's own inferno scale",
                "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202661023"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dust-2019",
              "label": "Great Dimming clump · December 2019",
              "thumbnail": "betelgeuse-lens-matisse.webp",
              "volume": {
                "objectId": "betelgeuse-shell",
                "lensId": "veil-2019-12",
                "surface": "matisse"
              },
              "notes": "The same photosphere, with the dust clump Montargès et al. (2021) fitted to the Great Dimming, drawn from the numbers they published for December 2019: a sphere of uniform density south and slightly west of the star and between it and us. Its extinction is scaled so the line of sight through its centre dims the star ten times, as they report for the southern hemisphere, and the light it scatters back is drawn faintly over that. A model fitted to images, not an image."
            },
            {
              "id": "sio-2023",
              "label": "SiO envelope · August 2023",
              "thumbnail": "betelgeuse-lens-matisse.webp",
              "falseColor": true,
              "volume": {
                "objectId": "betelgeuse-shell",
                "lensId": "sio-2023",
                "surface": "matisse"
              },
              "notes": "The same photosphere, with the silicon monoxide ALMA saw around it in August 2023. This is the only dataset here placed in depth by its own measured velocities rather than by a shape fitted to a picture. Everything is measured from the star itself, which the same observation's continuum places a tenth of an arcsecond, nearly five stellar radii, from where ALMA pointed. In front of the star the molecule absorbs its light, some of it leaving the star and some falling back; around it, the molecule glows in a lopsided ring between two and four stellar radii out, heaviest to the east-south-east. Each channel of the line cube is placed along the line of sight on a spherical outflow at the speed of the fastest gas absorbing in front of the star, about twenty-two kilometres a second, which is the usual first reading of an expanding stellar envelope. Gas that is falling back is placed as if it were flowing out, so the depths are a model. Silicon monoxide is what silicate dust condenses from, so this is the material of the other datasets caught before it became dust. The colours are a false-colour ramp for line brightness, not colour or temperature.",
